= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <implicit differentiation>, treating $y$ as a <function> of $x$:
$$
\boxed{4x+6y\frac{dy}{dx}=0.}
$$
Where $y\ne0$, this is $\boxed{dy/dx=-2x/(3y)}$. At the two points where $y=0$, the <ellipse> has vertical <tangent lines> rather than a finite slope.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The defining equation gives $y^2=2-(2/3)x^2\leq2$. Equality is attainable at $x=0$, with the positive branch giving
$$
\boxed{y_{\max}=\sqrt2\quad\text{at }(0,\sqrt2).}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At an intersection, $x^2=(x-1)^2=x^2-2x+1$, so $x=1/2$. Substitution into either <parabola> gives
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(\frac12,\frac14\right).}
$$
The resulting linear equation for $x$ has only one solution, so this is the unique intersection.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two <derivatives> are $2x$ and $2(x-1)$. At the intersection their slopes are $1$ and $-1$, so their direction <vectors> $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ have zero <dot product>. Thus the <tangent lines> are perpendicular and their angle is
$$
\boxed{\frac\pi2\ \text{radians}=90^\circ.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Multiply the <separable differential equation> by $e^y$: $e^y y'=e^x$. The left side is $(e^y)'$, hence $e^y=e^x+C$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\ln(e^x+C),\qquad e^x+C>0.}
$$
The real <general solution> is taken on any connected interval where the logarithm's argument is positive.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <initial condition> gives $e^{y(0)}=2=1+C$, so $C=1$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\ln(1+e^x),\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Its logarithm has positive argument for every real $x$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

At $x=3$, the point on the <parabola> is $(3,8)$ and the <tangent line> has slope $f'(3)=6$. The <normal line to a plane curve> therefore has slope $-1/6$. Its equation is
$$
y-8=-\frac16(x-3),\qquad y=-\frac x6+\frac{17}{2}.
$$
Thus the requested results are
$$
\boxed{\text{gradient }-\frac16,\qquad\text{vertical-axis intercept }(0,17/2).}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <tangent> has poles at $\pi/2$ and $3\pi/2$, so these are vertical <asymptotes> of $x\tan x$. The zeros on the given interval are $0,\pi,2\pi$. Near zero, $x\tan x=x^2+O(x^4)$, so the graph starts with a horizontal <tangent line>.

Each of its three branches is increasing. Indeed
$$
f'(x)=\frac{x+\sin x\cos x}{\cos^2x},
$$
and the numerator starts at zero with <derivative> $2\cos^2x\geq0$, so it is positive for $x>0$. The first branch rises from zero to $+\infty$, the middle branch rises from $-\infty$ through $(\pi,0)$ to $+\infty$, and the last rises from $-\infty$ to $(2\pi,0)$.

\Image[paper-1-tangent-circle.png]
{title=The three increasing tangent-product branches and their intersections with the right half of the circle}

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substitution into the <circle> gives $x^2\sec^2x=25$, or $x=5|\cos x|$ since $x\geq0$. Necessarily $x\leq5$, and each admissible $x$ fixes a unique $y=x\tan x$.

On $[0,\pi/2)$, $x-5\cos x$ is strictly increasing from a negative value to a positive value, giving one root. On $(\pi/2,3\pi/2)$, the equation is $x+5\cos x=0$. This <function> is strictly convex, positive at the two interval ends and negative at $\pi$, giving exactly two roots.

There are none on the remaining possible interval $3\pi/2<x\leq5$. Put $\delta=x-3\pi/2>0$; then $\cos x=\sin\delta\leq\delta$, so
$$
x-5\cos x\geq\frac{3\pi}{2}-4\delta
\geq\frac{15\pi}{2}-20>0.
$$
Hence the number of simultaneous real solutions is **$3$**, as marked in the preceding graph.

