= Solution
{parent=a}

For real powers work on $x>0$. Apply <integration by parts> with $u=\ln x$ and $dv=x^m dx$, so $v=x^{m+1}/(m+1)$ when $m\ne-1$. Then
$$
\boxed{\int x^m\ln x\,dx
=\frac{x^{m+1}\ln x}{m+1}-\frac{x^{m+1}}{(m+1)^2}+C.}
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $m=-1$, set $u=\ln x$, $du=dx/x$. This <integration by substitution> gives
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{\ln x}{x}\,dx=\frac12(\ln x)^2+C,\qquad x>0.}
$$
This case is treated separately because the denominator $m+1$ in part (a) vanishes.

