= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <parabola> opens upward, with its minimum at $(0,-1)$ and zeros at $x=\pm1$. The quartic graph is symmetric about the vertical axis, has its flat maximum at $(0,1)$, and also has zeros at $\pm1$.

Their intersections solve $x^4+x^2-2=(x^2-1)(x^2+2)=0$, so the only real intersections are $(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$. Between them, $1-x^4$ is the upper curve and $x^2-1$ the lower curve.

\Image[paper-1-parabola-quartic.png]
{title=The parabola, the inverted quartic, and the finite region between their intersections}

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Subtract the lower curve from the upper curve on the intersection interval. The <definite integral> is
$$
\mathcal A=\int_{-1}^1[(1-x^4)-(x^2-1)]dx
=2\int_0^1(2-x^2-x^4)dx
=\boxed{\frac{44}{15}}.
$$
The result is in square coordinate units.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Collect the coefficients of the three unknowns in rows. The <linear system> has <matrix> form
$$
\boxed{A\mathbf x=\mathbf b,\qquad
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\1&-2&1\\2&1&-1\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf b=\begin{pmatrix}14\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The next two parts solve this same system by the two requested methods.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <Gaussian elimination> on the <augmented matrix>. First take $R_2\leftarrow R_2-R_1$ and $R_3\leftarrow R_3-2R_1$, then $R_3\leftarrow4R_3-3R_2$:
$$
\left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&3&14\\1&-2&1&0\\2&1&-1&1\end{array}\right)
\longrightarrow
\left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&3&14\\0&-4&-2&-14\\0&-3&-7&-27\end{array}\right)
\longrightarrow
\left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&3&14\\0&-4&-2&-14\\0&0&-22&-66\end{array}\right).
$$
By <back substitution>, the last row gives $z=3$, the second gives $-4y-6=-14$ and hence $y=2$, and the first gives $x+4+9=14$. Thus
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(1,2,3).}
$$
The right-hand side has been transformed in every row operation, preserving the solution set.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose <Cramer's rule>, the permitted alternative to finding an inverse. The <determinant> is
$$
\Delta=1(2-1)-2(-1-2)+3(1+4)=22\ne0.
$$
Replacing each column by the right-hand side gives
$$
\Delta_x=\det\begin{pmatrix}14&2&3\\0&-2&1\\1&1&-1\end{pmatrix}
=14+2+6=22,
$$
$$
\Delta_y=\det\begin{pmatrix}1&14&3\\1&0&1\\2&1&-1\end{pmatrix}
=-1+42+3=44,
$$
$$
\Delta_z=\det\begin{pmatrix}1&2&14\\1&-2&0\\2&1&1\end{pmatrix}
=-2-2+70=66.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{x=\Delta_x/\Delta=1,\quad y=\Delta_y/\Delta=2,\quad z=\Delta_z/\Delta=3.}
$$
The nonzero <determinant> also proves uniqueness.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The square roots simplify to the <absolute values> $|a-b|$ and $|a+b|$. Put $s=\sin(\pi\sqrt{a^2+b^2})$. A nontrivial solution of the homogeneous <linear system> exists exactly when its <coefficient matrix> is singular:
$$
0=\det\begin{pmatrix}|a-b|&s\\|a+b|&s\end{pmatrix}
=s(|a-b|-|a+b|).
$$
The second factor vanishes precisely when $(a-b)^2=(a+b)^2$, or $ab=0$, giving both coordinate axes. The first factor vanishes when $\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ is a nonnegative integer. Hence the complete <locus> is
$$
\boxed{\{a=0\}\ \cup\ \{b=0\}\ \cup\
\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\{a^2+b^2=n^2\}.}
$$
These are the two axes together with concentric <circles> of positive integer radii. The zero-radius case is the origin, already included on the axes; at that point both equations vanish and any nonzero pair $(x,y)$ works.

\Image[paper-1-singular-system-locus.png]
{title=The parameter locus consists of both axes and circles with integer radii}

