= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If either <vector> is zero, both sides vanish. Otherwise let $\theta$ be the angle between them. The magnitudes of the <cross product> and <dot product> are described by
$$
|\mathbf a\times\mathbf b|=|\mathbf a|\,|\mathbf b|\sin\theta,
\qquad \mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=|\mathbf a|\,|\mathbf b|\cos\theta.
$$
Squaring and using $\sin^2\theta=1-\cos^2\theta$ proves <Lagrange's identity>:
$$
\boxed{|\mathbf a\times\mathbf b|^2
=|\mathbf a|^2|\mathbf b|^2-(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume the two line-defining points are distinct. Put $\mathbf u=\mathbf r_1-\mathbf r_0$ and $\mathbf v=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_0$. The <triangle> formed by the three points has area $|\mathbf u\times\mathbf v|/2$. The same area is half the base length $|\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1|$ times the perpendicular height $D$ from the third point to the line. Hence the <distance from a point to a line in three dimensions> is
$$
\boxed{D=\frac{|(\mathbf r_1-\mathbf r_0)\times(\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_0)|}
{|\mathbf r_1-\mathbf r_2|}.}
$$
This remains valid when the nearest point lies outside the segment, since the base line is infinite. If all three points are collinear, the numerator and distance are both zero.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use $(0,1,1)$ as an anchor. Two directions in the required <plane> are
$$
\mathbf u=(2,0,2),\qquad \mathbf v=(4,1,0).
$$
Their <cross product> is $(-2,8,2)$, so a <normal vector> is $\mathbf n=(-1,4,1)$. The <Cartesian equation of a plane> is
$$
\mathbf n\cdot[(x,y,z)-(0,1,1)]=0,
\qquad \boxed{-x+4y+z=5.}
$$
Direct substitution verifies all three given points. The independent direction <vectors> ensure the <plane> is uniquely determined.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The axis intercepts of the <plane> are
$$
P=(-5,0,0),\qquad Q=(0,5/4,0),\qquad R=(0,0,5).
$$
Use two edge <vectors> from $P$:
$$
(Q-P)\times(R-P)
=(5,5/4,0)\times(5,0,5)=(25/4,-25,-25/4).
$$
The <triangle> area is half this <cross product> magnitude:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A=\frac12\frac{25}{4}\sqrt{1+16+1}
=\frac{75\sqrt2}{8}.}
$$

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In <cylindrical coordinates>, $x=r\cos\theta$ and $y=r\sin\theta$, so $x^2+y^2=r^2$. Thus the circular <elliptic paraboloid> has equation
$$
\boxed{az=r^2,\quad\text{or}\quad z=r^2/a.}
$$
At height $h\geq0$, the rim <radius> is $\sqrt{ah}$.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $h>0$, parametrize the curved surface by $(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta,r^2/a)$, with $0\leq r\leq\sqrt{ah}$ and $0\leq\theta\leq2\pi$. The magnitude of the <cross product> of its coordinate tangents gives the <area element of a surface>:
$$
dS=r\sqrt{1+\frac{4r^2}{a^2}}\,dr\,d\theta.
$$
Integrating gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
S&=2\pi\int_0^{\sqrt{ah}}r\sqrt{1+4r^2/a^2}\,dr\\
&=\frac{\pi a^2}{6}\left[(1+4r^2/a^2)^{3/2}\right]_0^{\sqrt{ah}}\\
&=\boxed{\frac{\pi a^2}{6}\left[\left(1+\frac{4h}{a}\right)^{3/2}-1\right].}
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the curved <surface area>, not the area of a lid across the horizontal opening. The expression tends to zero at $h=0$ and has dimensions of area. The <volume and centroid of a parabolic cup> use the same geometry.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The liquid occupies $0\leq z\leq h$ and $0\leq r\leq\sqrt{az}$. A horizontal slice is a disk of area $\pi az$. Integrating these areas gives
$$
\boxed{V=\int_0^h\pi az\,dz=\frac{\pi ah^2}{2}.}
$$
Equivalently, the <triple integral> is $\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^h\int_0^{\sqrt{az}}r\,dr\,dz\,d\theta$, including the cylindrical <Jacobian determinant> factor $r$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For positive constant <mass density> $\rho_0$, reflection symmetry makes the horizontal coordinates of the <centre of mass> zero. The total mass and the vertical first moment are
$$
M=\rho_0\frac{\pi ah^2}{2},\qquad
\int z\rho_0\,dV=\rho_0\int_0^h z(\pi az)dz
=\frac{\rho_0\pi ah^3}{3}.
$$
Their ratio gives
$$
\boxed{(\bar x,\bar y,\bar z)=(0,0,2h/3).}
$$
This is the <centre> of the liquid <volume>, rather than of the curved wall. The density cancels because it is uniform; the larger disks near the top place the <centre> above half-height.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <gradient> factors as
$$
f_x=y(3x^2+y^2-1),\qquad f_y=x(x^2+3y^2-1).
$$
If a coordinate is zero, the solutions are $(0,0)$, $(\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\pm1)$. With both coordinates nonzero, subtracting the two bracket equations gives $x^2=y^2$, hence $x^2=y^2=1/4$. These give four more <stationary points>.

The <Hessian matrix> has equal diagonal entries $6xy$ and off-diagonal entry $3(x^2+y^2)-1$. At the origin its <eigenvalues> are $\pm1$, and at the four nonzero axis points they are $\pm2$, so all five are saddles. At the two same-sign interior points the <eigenvalues> are $1,2$, while at the opposite-sign points they are $-1,-2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{points}&\text{type}&f\\\hline
(0,0),(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)&\text{saddles}&0\\
(1/2,1/2),(-1/2,-1/2)&\text{local minima}&-1/8\\
(1/2,-1/2),(-1/2,1/2)&\text{local maxima}&1/8.
\end{array}}
$$
The extrema are not global, since the quartic takes arbitrarily large positive and negative values along suitable rays.

For the contour sketch, the zero level is exactly the two axes together with the unit <circle>. Inside the <circle>, $f$ is negative in the first and third quadrants and positive in the other two; outside, those signs reverse. Small negative levels contain closed loops around the two minima as well as unbounded branches in the second and fourth quadrants. Small positive levels similarly contain two loops around the maxima and unbounded branches in the first and third quadrants. The closed loops shrink to the extrema at levels $\mp1/8$, and disappear beyond those levels. This is the unit-scale <quartic surface with axes and a circle as its zero set>.

\Image[paper-1-quartic-contours.png]
{title=The quartic's nine stationary points and its zero set of axes and a unit circle}

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a classical solution take $F,G$ twice differentiable, so that the second <derivatives> exist. Direct use of the <chain rule> gives
$$
y_{tt}=25(F''+G''),\qquad y_{xx}=4(F''+G''),
$$
with the two profile arguments as specified. Thus $4y_{tt}=25y_{xx}$.

To prove generality, introduce <characteristic coordinates> $\xi=2x+5t$, $\eta=2x-5t$, whose Jacobian <determinant> is $-20\ne0$. For $y=U(\xi,\eta)$ the differential operator is
$$
4\partial_t^2-25\partial_x^2=-400\partial_\xi\partial_\eta.
$$
The equation becomes $U_{\xi\eta}=0$. Integrating once in each variable yields
$$
\boxed{U(\xi,\eta)=F(\xi)+G(\eta).}
$$
This is the <countertravelling decomposition of the one-dimensional wave equation>, with <wave speed> $5/2$. Once differentiability alone is not sufficient for a classical second-order equation; the displayed argument uses the required second <derivatives>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose $F(s)=G(s)=\tfrac12\sin s$. The two travelling profiles then add by the <sine> addition identity:
$$
\boxed{y(x,t)=\frac12\sin(2x+5t)+\frac12\sin(2x-5t)
=\sin2x\cos5t.}
$$
This is a <standing wave>. At $x=0$ and $x=\pi$ it vanishes for all $t$. At $t=0$ its displacement is $\sin2x$, and its time <derivative> $-5\sin2x\sin5t$ is zero. Also $y_{tt}=-25y$ and $y_{xx}=-4y$, directly checking the original equation.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
